{"t":1789309544,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"accdc7d4-f399-4811-a426-16a9559cc433","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-13T13:33:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 131333\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n933 AM EDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nSlightly expanded the mention of isolated showers and possibly a\nthunderstorm this afternoon.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Some lingering showers today before turning cooler with \n  less humidity on Monday and Tuesday.\n\n- 2) Warm and dry conditions through the end of the week;\n  showers possible along a cold front next weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some lingering showers today before turning \ncooler with less humidity on Monday and Tuesday.\n\nWhile much of the remainder of the day will be dry, a pre-\nfrontal trough may help induce a few additional showers or\nthunderstorms, but these should be rather disorganized and\nisolated in nature. By the afternoon, expect more sun than \nclouds which will help crank temperatures back well into the \n80s. This is aided by a weak downslope component to the winds as\nthey shift to northwesterly. Despite the wind shift, humidity \nlevels do remain elevated with dew points in the upper 60s to \nlow 70s.\n\nHeading into tonight, an approaching cold front from the Great\nLakes may induce additional showers along and west of the\nAllegheny Front. Otherwise, this is largely a drier frontal\npassage which will also lead to cool temperatures and lower\nhumidity to start off the work week. Gradients are rather tight\nfor mid-September as a broad 1024-mb surface high approaches\nfrom the Great Lakes. This will favor a breezy northwesterly\nwind for Monday, with afternoon gusts up to 20 to 25 mph at\ntimes. Forecast highs are in the mid 70s to low 80s (5 to 15\ndegrees cooler for mountain locales). As the seasonably strong\ndome of high pressure settles over the northeastern U.S. on\nMonday night, low temperatures plunge into the upper 40s to 50s.\nThe autumnal feel to the air lingers into Tuesday before an\neventual warm up by mid-week. Plenty of sunshine can be expected\neach day, but with decreasing winds on Tuesday with the ridge\nalong the Eastern Seaboard. This all comes with dew points in\nthe upper 40s to mid 50s which is certainly a change from recent\ndays.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warm and dry conditions through the end of the\nweek; showers possible along a cold front next weekend.\n\nA strong upper ridge builds over the region during the middle \nportion of the week supporting very warm and dry conditions. Not\nlooking at any record highs since records are in the mid 90s. \nThe ridge begins to flatten at the end of the week as it starts \nfeeling pressure from a deepening trough over Hudson Bay. A \nfront eventually sinks southward at the end of the week possibly\nbringing few-sct showers. Overall, very warm and dry this week,\nturning cooler and potentially wetter for late September.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /12Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nMVFR/IFR ceilings persist through the mid/late morning before \nimprovements to VFR take shape by midday. Thereafter, clouds \nshould continue to erode which allows for some sun to break out.\nA pop up shower or storm can't be ruled out this afternoon.\n\nVFR conditions then continue through Tuesday. Winds remain weak\ntoday, generally out of the west to northwest. A seasonably \nstrong cold front tracks through early Monday which yields post-\nfrontal northwesterlies with gusts to around 20 to perhaps 25 \nknots. Winds eventually weaken into Tuesday as high pressure \nbuilds over the region.\n\nFair weather conditions expected through Thursday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nA pop up shower or storm can't be ruled out this afternoon or\nearly evening. A cold front arriving early Monday is forecast \nto yield increasing northwesterly winds with gusts up to 20 to \n25 knots. Small Craft Advisories look quite likely on Monday \ninto portions of Monday night. Multiple wind shifts take place \non Tuesday as high pressure passes by to the north. However, \ngusts drop off back into the 5 to 10 knot range.\n\nSub-advisory conditions continue into Wednesday and Thursday \nwith gusts to around 5 to 10 knots.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/BRO/DHOF\nAVIATION...LFR/BRO/DHOF\nMARINE...LFR/BRO/DHOF\n"},"forecast":{"t":1789309520,"days":{"2026-09-13":{"hi":87,"lo":71,"pop":20,"short":"Partly Sunny then Isolated Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-14":{"hi":81,"lo":57,"pop":3,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-15":{"hi":81,"lo":60,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-16":{"hi":85,"lo":65,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-17":{"hi":87,"lo":67,"pop":12,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-18":{"hi":88,"lo":67,"pop":29,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-19":{"hi":84,"lo":65,"pop":36,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1789308000,"n":48,"ceil":[2000,2800,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[9.3,9.8,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.9,9.9,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[5,6,5,5,5,5,5,5,5,5,5,4,4,4,4,5,5,6,7,9,11,12,13,13,13,13,13,13,13,13,13,12,12,10,7,6,5,5,5,5,5,5,4,4,4,4,4,4],"gst":[8,10,10,10,10,10,10,10,10,9,9,9,9,9,10,10,10,13,15,18,19,21,22,21,21,21,20,20,20,21,21,20,18,17,13,12,12,11,11,11,11,11,10,10,10,9,9,8],"dir":[260,280,290,290,260,240,220,220,210,200,200,200,230,250,260,270,280,290,310,320,340,350,350,0,0,350,35